# Line endings.
#
# Every text blob in this repo is already stored LF in the index (362 of 362 at
# the time this file was added), but nothing enforced it. On a Windows checkout
# with core.autocrlf left at its default the working tree came back CRLF, git
# reported the entire tree as modified, and every diff arrived with ~360 files
# of pure line-ending noise sitting on top of the actual change.
#
# That is not cosmetic. A review that has to be filtered before it can be read
# is a review that gets skimmed, and this repo's whole premise is that the
# gates are actually looked at. It also makes `git add -A` a live grenade: it
# sweeps 360 unrelated files into a commit that was meant to touch three.
#
# text=auto  -> git decides text vs binary per file, normalises text to LF in
#               the index (already true, now enforced)
# eol=lf     -> and checks it out as LF on every platform, so the working tree
#               matches the index regardless of anyone's local git config
#
# No renormalisation commit is needed: the index is already clean.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Binary. Listed explicitly rather than left to the heuristic — a mis-detected
# asset gets silently corrupted by text conversion, and the failure shows up as
# a broken image months later rather than as a diff.
*.png   binary
*.gif   binary
*.jpg   binary
*.jpeg  binary
*.ico   binary
*.skill binary

# Two source files carry LITERAL NUL BYTES as security fixtures, and must never
# be run through any text filter:
#
#   scripts/ci-check-pr-separation.js  line 216 tests that a path containing a
#       NUL is rejected; lines 615-616 are the fixtures that exercise it
#       ("scripts/scenario.js\0.md" — the classic extension-spoofing payload)
#   tests/watcher/sonnet/run-tests.js  line 187 feeds a NUL-bearing path to the
#       watcher's projectRoot
#
# git's auto-detection already classifies both as binary today, which is why
# they have survived. But `text=auto` re-runs that heuristic on every future
# revision: edit either file enough that the NUL/byte ratio shifts and the
# heuristic can flip to "text", at which point a filter is free to touch it.
#
# Marking them binary explicitly removes that path entirely. It costs a
# non-diffable file in review, which is the correct trade for a fixture whose
# entire value is that one specific byte survives.
scripts/ci-check-pr-separation.js binary
tests/watcher/sonnet/run-tests.js binary
